00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:16.019
that we're aiming to develop.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:19.720
So again, I'll give an overview
of information retrieval,

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.269
introduce the specific topics.

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:24.460
And then the secondary aim
is to actually demonstrate

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:27.909
what one can do with open
educational resources.

00:04:27.909 --> 00:04:33.409
In fact, essentially what I've
done for this lecture is pull

00:04:33.409 --> 00:04:36.639
slides from two of our
modules-- one on information

00:04:36.639 --> 00:04:39.659
retrieval, one on ontologies.

00:04:39.659 --> 00:04:42.449
And then I've modified
them to fit this.

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:45.490
And that's the purpose of
open educational resources

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.650
is for people to be
able to, not only

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:52.940
use the materials to learn
from, but also to teach.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:57.510
And under a Creative
Commons kind of license.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:01.930
So let me start then with data
and information retrieval.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.729
I'll show you some
figures that I

00:05:04.729 --> 00:05:08.740
think give a perspective
on what we're

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:12.139
doing when we're trying to
do information retrieval--

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:13.720
the process, the
knowledge discovery,

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:17.550
and then some of the
challenges in the modern era

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.449
for information retrieval.

00:05:20.449 --> 00:05:25.560
So I like to think-- I
know that last week Dr.Musen

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.819
talked about
the lifecycle of data.

00:05:28.819 --> 00:05:30.530
I actually have a similar.

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:33.280
This figure actually
comes from my book.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.050
For me to try to figure
out what it is we're

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:39.560
retrieving when we're looking up
scientific information and now

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.740
increasingly data.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:46.410
So most scientific knowledge
is generated through research,

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:47.800
usually new research.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.789
In this day and age,
research can not only

00:05:49.789 --> 00:05:53.900
be things in labs, things
in clinical trials,

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:57.969
but of course re-analysis
of data-- things

00:05:57.969 --> 00:06:00.610
like systematic reviews
and metaanalysis

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:04.180
or going into either
biological or clinical data

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:06.860
and doing further analysis.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.090
If we want to publicize
that research,

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.849
we write up the
results into a paper,

00:06:11.849 --> 00:06:14.860
submit it for publication
to, usually, a journal

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:18.509
or conference proceedings, goes
through a peer review process.

00:06:18.509 --> 00:06:22.389
It may be rejected, and you
have to revise it and resubmit.

00:06:22.389 --> 00:06:25.250
And sometimes you get caught
in that cycle quite a bit.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.629
But if you're persistent and
you have decent research,

00:06:27.629 --> 00:06:29.919
it will eventually be
accepted, and then you'll

00:06:29.919 --> 00:06:33.029
publish it, typically in
a journal, although that

00:06:33.029 --> 00:06:35.919
may be changing over time.

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:40.509
When you do publish it, you
see some dotted lines here

00:06:40.509 --> 00:06:42.930
of things that may happen.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.949
So you may actually have
to relinquish copyright.

00:06:45.949 --> 00:06:47.449
That's a big issue.

00:06:47.449 --> 00:06:49.270
That could probably take
an hour in and of itself.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:52.539
I won't address in today's talk.

00:06:52.539 --> 00:06:55.520
Your work may make it into
secondary publications--

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.770
reviews, text books,
things like that.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:00.879
And of course, if your
research is any good,

00:07:00.879 --> 00:07:05.259
it will generate new questions
that need to be asked,

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:08.129
or it will generate
data that others

00:07:08.129 --> 00:07:11.740
may want to further analyze.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:16.020
These days, particularly
biological, like genomic data,

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:18.499
tends to go into
data repositories,

00:07:18.499 --> 00:07:20.211
less so clinical
data, though that

00:07:20.211 --> 00:07:22.150
will be changing over time.

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:25.930
So, historically, the focus
of information retrieval

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:29.370
has been to look
for those documents.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:32.319
But now, of course, there
is the potential ability

00:07:32.319 --> 00:07:35.219
to actually find [it].

00:07:35.219 --> 00:07:39.249
The way I look at information
retrieval is as a funnel--

00:07:39.249 --> 00:07:41.909
and, again, this
comes from my book--

00:07:41.909 --> 00:07:44.759
where the process of
information retrieval

00:07:44.759 --> 00:07:47.349
is where we really are searching
over all the literature,

00:07:47.349 --> 00:07:50.259
like when we go PubMed,
and in the process,

00:07:50.259 --> 00:07:53.059
we find some possibly relevant
literature with our search.

00:07:53.059 --> 00:07:55.879
And then we select what's
definitely relevant.

00:07:55.879 --> 00:07:58.520
But, of course, we may
want to do more with that.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:01.539
We may want to
actually do text mining

00:08:01.539 --> 00:08:03.949
on the descriptions
in the papers,

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:05.729
or we may want to
get at the tables

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:08.059
or even ultimately to the data.

00:08:08.059 --> 00:08:11.449
We may want structured
or actionable knowledge.

00:08:11.449 --> 00:08:14.240
That's more the domain of
information extraction and text

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.240
mining.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:19.289
Those processes usually make use
of natural language processing,

00:08:19.289 --> 00:08:23.120
machine learning,
other kinds of things.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:26.089
The information retrieval
is-- I wouldn't call it

00:08:26.089 --> 00:08:28.309
a solved problem--
but it's a problem

00:08:28.309 --> 00:08:31.599
that there is a lot of
widespread, commercialized

00:08:31.599 --> 00:08:35.940
solutions, whereas information
extraction text mining

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:40.200
is something that is
starting to emerge.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.370
There are some
commercial companies,

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:44.870
but there's also a lot
of research systems

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:46.980
that do that, as well.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:50.950
So focusing in on the process
of information retrieval, what

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:53.760
we're ultimately aiming
to find is content.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.100
And I use that word
"content" deliberately

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:01.310
to really cast the net broadly--
anything from papers to images,

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:06.250
videos, and of course, data.

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:08.110
In order to be able
to find that content,

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:10.090
we have to go through
an indexing process.

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:11.900
I'll talk about that in
more detail in a moment,

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:16.020
some way of basically generating
metadata, data that describes

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:17.990
data about the content.

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:20.560
So when we have
that metadata, we

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:23.810
can then have users who
are seeking to retrieve

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.960
things, enter queries.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:30.130
And they enter queries in the
metadata and a search engine—

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:32.540
and I'm sure you're all
familiar with search engines

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:37.490
like Google and
PubMed-- will match

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:42.110
that metadata from the
queries to the content,

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:46.870
so the user can retrieve.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:48.150
Information retrieval,
when I started

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:50.470
working in this
field in the 1980s

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:52.180
was something that
you oftentimes

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:56.020
had to go to a librarian
and or used to pay money

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:59.370
for dial-up internet
or to dial up actually

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:02.830
into the systems themselves
over time sharing

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:05.280
networks, CompuServe, AOL.

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:09.480
Now of course, all of us
have computers and phones,

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:13.970
and essentially everyone
has used a search engine.

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:17.560
In fact, some browsers,
like Apple Safari,

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:22.090
will-- if what you type into
the URL box doesn't match a URL,

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:24.510
it'll throw you into
a search engine.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:29.010
And people search for
health-related information.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:33.210
And the latest statistic,
71% of internet users,

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:37.830
meaning 59% of all adults
search for information, 35%

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:38.830
using it for self-diagnosis.

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:42.060
And of course, this is
not just a US phenomenon.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:46.450
It comes from the entire world.

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:49.410
Search is considered an
integral application.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:54.470
Many software packages have
Search built into them.

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:56.810
Enterprises develop
Search capabilities

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:59.930
across their assets
and so forth.

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:03.460
And we even have search
engine optimization,

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:05.060
something those of
us doing research

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:06.700
in the early days
never thought of,

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:08.670
where people would be
willing to pay money

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:14.150
to have their search results
move up higher in the rankings.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:17.240
Fortunately, some of us
have last names that are not

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:19.610
that common like H-E-R-S-H.

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:22.450
So I, fortunately, have
never had to pay money

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:26.330
to have my rankings elevated.

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:28.900
If you type my name into
Google, I come right at the top.

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:31.210
And just to show you how
mainstream-- I'm sure many,

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:35.610
if not all of you, have been
so these search systems.

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:38.580
PubMed and Google are
probably the-- PubMed

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:44.750
for anything biomedical
and Google for anything.

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:48.220
We also know that
access to information

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:52.460
is really part and parcel of
health and biomedicine now.

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:56.030
InCell, back in 2003, said
that biology's becoming

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:57.470
an information science.

00:11:57.470 --> 00:12:01.720
The old days of capturing things
in your laboratory notebook

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:05.030
is being replaced by
databases and spreadsheets

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.280
and all other kinds of things.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:09.680
We hear about pharmaceutical
companies competing

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:11.630
for informatics talent.

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:15.110
On the clinical side,
we have clinicians

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:18.900
trying to keep up with the
knowledge of their field, where

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:22.130
they're in an era when there
is about 75 clinical trials

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:25.590
and 11 systematic reviews
published each day.

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:27.600
And searching is ubiquitous.

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:29.800
It's even part of--
for those of you

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:31.710
who are familiar with
electronic health records--

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:36.320
the meaningful use regulations.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:39.160
And one of the
requirements is the ability

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:42.310
to do a text search over
electronic health record notes.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:45.810
So searching is pretty common.

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.050
But we have some challenges.

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:50.740
We perhaps, 20, 30
years ago, there

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:53.020
was not that much
information online.

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:55.320
Now we had the opposite problem.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:56.710
We're overloaded.

00:12:56.710 --> 00:13:00.780
We have challenges searching
because there are multiple ways

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:07.470
to say the names of diseases,
the names of genes, symptoms.

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:09.000
Searching can be a
challenge when there's

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:11.150
so much information out there.

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:13.140
Of course, there is also
the converse problems,

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.050
where we have many
words and terms that

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:17.850
have multiple meanings.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:19.350
The word "cold."

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:22.750
It could be the temperature
cold, the disease cold,

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:24.090
and so forth.

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:26.810
Or there was just
paper in I think

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.590
it was Nature within the
last few weeks about how--

00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:36.220
or maybe it was TLOS-- but
how spreadsheets can mess up

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:38.880
genes analyses because
genes like apr-1

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:44.430
are converted by things like
Excel into the date April 1st.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:47.200
So then, of course, apr-1
has multiple meanings.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:48.900
And then this other
issue-- again, which

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:52.880
is a very interesting topic to
me and probably many of you,

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:54.690
but it's just beyond
the scope of today--

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:58.150
is how we balance that
open access, making

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.120
the fruits of
research, most of which

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:03.410
are paid for by the
government, freely available

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:06.660
versus the real cost of
producing it and maintaining

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:07.660
it.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:09.430
So these are some of the
challenges we'll face.

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:13.450
I'm not going to solve
all these problems today.

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:18.050
So let me drill a
little further into some

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:19.800
of the aspects of
information retrieval.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:21.260
Let me start with content.

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:23.710
And, again, I use
that word "content"

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:25.920
in a real broad sort of way.

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:33.290
So back in the 1960s,
70s, even into the 1980s,

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:34.290
networks were slow.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:36.310
We mostly connected
by telephone modems.

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:38.450
Discs didn't have
that much capability.

00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:40.350
So most of the
content we search on

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:42.890
was abstracts, and mostly
of journal articles.

00:14:42.890 --> 00:14:45.950
But then, as the technology
improved, the network bandwidth

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:49.590
improved, the whole system
became more affordable

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:54.430
for individuals and
organizations to connect to.

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:56.600
And these aren't really
in timewise order,

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:58.680
but they're sort of.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:00.750
We then moved to full text.

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:02.560
So instead of the
abstracts now, we

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:05.410
could put the whole
article online.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:07.090
We could move things
like text books.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:08.700
We could publish reports.

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:11.640
And so those are
predominantly text based.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.790
Still the memory and bandwidth
requirements are modest.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:17.540
But then we started
moving into hypermedia,

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:19.700
where we have web
pages, images, sounds,

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:21.700
and videos, and so forth.

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:26.120
And the bandwidth requirements
and storage requirements

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:27.120
started to increase.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:29.660
And then also the development
of structured selection--

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:33.580
textbooks that are linked across
each other, large databases,

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:35.490
compendiums, and so forth.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:38.730
And so these-- the
middle bullet--

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:43.280
those content materials have
been increasing in size.

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:45.840
And in fact, we pretty
much take them for granted.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.260
And then now we move
into the new era,

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:50.790
when we can no
longer just publish

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:55.590
the results of our research,
but we can publish our data.

00:15:55.590 --> 00:16:01.690
And if you read the medical
literature or any other source,

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:05.830
there's controversies
about how readily available

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:07.830
should data be.

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:10.560
Someone coining the term
research parasites, which

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:15.890
made a lot of people upset.

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:18.810
What are the issues behind
making data available?

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:22.340
I won't comment on the
economic or political issues,

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:26.670
but I will comment on the
technology and informatics

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:32.310
issues around data retrieval.

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:33.540
So I talked about content.

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.020
Let me talk a little
about indexing.

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:38.160
Indexing is critical
because we need somehow

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:42.940
to be able to find the
content we're looking for.

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:44.570
And this is where
metadata comes in.

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:46.550
Data about data.

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:48.880
We assign metadata
to content items,

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:54.040
and we can do it in automated
fashions or using manual

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:55.220
methods.

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:59.590
Automated systems, things like
Google, things like the word

00:16:59.590 --> 00:17:02.810
search in PubMed,
will very easily

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:10.629
pull out the words in the
records or the documents

00:17:10.629 --> 00:17:11.629
and so forth.

00:17:11.629 --> 00:17:14.030
And we can assign
measures to them that

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:16.980
allow us to rank the output.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:21.610
Any undergraduate
computer science student

00:17:21.610 --> 00:17:25.790
can write algorithms to extract
out the words of a document.

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:28.970
It's a relatively
known thing to do.

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:32.169
We've also, since the beginning
of things like Medline,

00:17:32.169 --> 00:17:35.950
had manual indexing,
where there are experts

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:42.820
who, in the case of Medline,
follow a protocol where they

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:46.059
assign terms from a
controlled vocabulary,

00:17:46.059 --> 00:17:49.000
in this case MeSH of the
medical subject headings,

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:50.419
and they follow a
protocol where they

00:17:50.419 --> 00:17:53.789
look at different parts of
the article in a process that

00:17:53.789 --> 00:17:57.340
takes about 15 to 20 minutes
because there are so many.

00:17:57.340 --> 00:17:59.330
So they focus on things
like the introduction

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:01.870
and the conclusion and
the figure legends.

00:18:01.870 --> 00:18:05.190
And then they assign anywhere
from 5 to 10 MeSH terms,

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:09.690
and a few of them get
designated as central concepts.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:14.350
So those are assigning subjects,
what the content is about.

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:16.169
But there's other
attributes we might want.

00:18:16.169 --> 00:18:18.610
For example, we might
want to know the author.

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:22.299
I actually do a fair
amount of author searching.

00:18:22.299 --> 00:18:25.370
Oftentimes, I know I want
to go find someone's paper

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:27.070
that was published
in a certain place,

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:29.000
so I enter their author name.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:34.150
And in PubMed, when you
type two words together

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:36.370
and the second word only
has one or two characters,

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:39.480
it will try to map
that into an author.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:41.770
We might want to know the
source, the journal that it

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:42.770
came from it.

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:46.699
It was a report that's published
by an institution, et cetera.

00:18:46.699 --> 00:18:48.529
We might want to know
the publication type.

00:18:48.529 --> 00:18:51.700
This is particularly important,
well, in a number of places,

00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:54.379
but one of the major
ones is with clinicians

00:18:54.379 --> 00:18:57.980
who are looking, for example, to
make an evidence based decision

00:18:57.980 --> 00:19:01.629
and use a randomized
control trial.

00:19:01.629 --> 00:19:05.059
There's things like secondary
source, in the case of Medline,

00:19:05.059 --> 00:19:06.929
and this is where
we have linkage

00:19:06.929 --> 00:19:11.929
to gene resources, protein
resources, all the other myriad

00:19:11.929 --> 00:19:15.840
of databases that are
available through the NLM,

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:17.179
the National
Library of Medicine,

00:19:17.179 --> 00:19:19.230
and other organizations.

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:23.679
We may want to know the grant
number for whatever reason.

00:19:23.679 --> 00:19:24.970
And then, of course,
the location.

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:27.419
The bibliographic
databases in the old days

00:19:27.419 --> 00:19:29.320
told us where to
go into the library

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:32.019
stacks to find the journal.

00:19:32.019 --> 00:19:37.690
Now the location gives us
a URL or basically a DOI,

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:42.200
a digital object identifier,
that's converted into a URL.

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:45.110
So right from the Medline
record, we can click a link

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:47.700
and go to the
publisher's website

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:51.110
to retrieve the article.

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:54.480
And sometimes we run into
paywalls and so forth.

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:57.740
But at least there's a link
to where we can go get it.

00:19:57.740 --> 00:19:59.919
We don't have to go
searching through the stacks

00:19:59.919 --> 00:20:02.169
in the days of old.

00:20:02.169 --> 00:20:04.500
A little bit more about MeSH.

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:07.750
MeSH-- well, all
indexing vocabularies,

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:10.470
they usually consist
of control terms.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:12.320
They're usually
organized in a hierarchy.

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:14.360
I have a figure on this slide.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:20.269
It's basically a vertical
slice through a piece of MeSH.

00:20:20.269 --> 00:20:25.459
MeSH has 16 trees, anatomy,
diseases, chemicals, and drugs,

00:20:25.459 --> 00:20:27.970
one of which is diseases.

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:31.309
And you see, I'm basically
traversing down the hierarchy--

00:20:31.309 --> 00:20:34.649
cardiovascular diseases,
vascular diseases,

00:20:34.649 --> 00:20:36.869
hypertension, et cetera.

00:20:36.869 --> 00:20:41.460
And all in all, there's
about 26,000 terms in MeSH,

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:42.940
and then there's a
number of synonyms.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:45.399
So you can express the
terms in different ways.

00:20:45.399 --> 00:20:47.200
And if you ever want
to do a look at MeSH,

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:53.950
there's a nice website that the
NLM keeps as basically a MeSH.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:54.950
browser.

00:20:54.950 --> 00:20:56.260
Onto the retrieval side.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:21:01.240
Remember the purpose of doing
indexing is so we can retrieve.

00:21:01.240 --> 00:21:03.399
And there is really two
kind of general approaches

00:21:03.399 --> 00:21:06.090
that search systems use.

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:10.220
One is the requirement for
Boolean operators—“and,”

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:12.840
“or,” and “not.”

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:15.700
“And” helping you to
narrow your set,

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:17.760
“Or” to express things
in different ways.

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:20.409
“Not” is less frequently
used, but sometimes we

00:21:20.409 --> 00:21:23.720
use it to filter
out certain things.

00:21:23.720 --> 00:21:27.679
Or what we see more commonly
is natural language systems,

00:21:27.679 --> 00:21:31.289
where we don't actually have
to explicitly type the Boolean

00:21:31.289 --> 00:21:33.919
operators, although
oftentimes things are done.

00:21:33.919 --> 00:21:36.509
For example, in
Google, the default

00:21:36.509 --> 00:21:38.919
is to do an “and” between
all of the words

00:21:38.919 --> 00:21:40.570
that you type into Google.

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:43.269
There's ways to override
that, but that's

00:21:43.269 --> 00:21:47.749
Google's basic function.

00:21:47.749 --> 00:21:52.370
The Boolean systems
basically give us sets.

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:54.379
Sources like PubMed
will sort them

00:21:54.379 --> 00:21:58.210
by reverse chronological order.

00:21:58.210 --> 00:22:00.619
With natural language
systems, we usually

00:22:00.619 --> 00:22:02.299
get the results ranked.

00:22:02.299 --> 00:22:04.769
And so when you do a
Google search or really

00:22:04.769 --> 00:22:08.070
any system that uses
natural language searching,

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:10.070
the results of
the search will be

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:13.409
sorted, sometimes by the word
occurrence and frequencies,

00:22:13.409 --> 00:22:16.759
of words that are common to
what the user has entered

00:22:16.759 --> 00:22:20.220
and what's in the content,
or the use of in-links, which

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:22.539
of course was popularized.

00:22:22.539 --> 00:22:26.650
It really was the basis of
the Google search engine

00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:29.509
and is part of the
reason why it works

00:22:29.509 --> 00:22:33.100
so well in the setting
of the World Wide Web

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:37.399
with billions of documents--
how many pages point to you.

00:22:37.399 --> 00:22:39.590
And that's one of
the things that

00:22:39.590 --> 00:22:41.610
goes into how Google
ranks its results.

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.519
So over the years,
they've added a lot more,

00:22:44.519 --> 00:22:46.429
and now it's all proprietary.

00:22:46.429 --> 00:22:48.620
But the original
feature of Google

00:22:48.620 --> 00:22:50.759
was that page rank
algorithm that

00:22:50.759 --> 00:22:54.450
was described by Brin in 1998.

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:57.650
A few more comments
about metadata,

00:22:57.650 --> 00:22:59.010
which is data about data.

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:02.090
So it tells us what
the data items mean.

00:23:02.090 --> 00:23:03.690
There's different
types of metadata.

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:06.590
We've mostly been talking
about descriptive metadata

00:23:06.590 --> 00:23:10.120
that enables us to
discover or identify it.

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:13.090
But there's also structural
metadata that describes how

00:23:13.090 --> 00:23:18.221
the data itself the
structure, the fields that

00:23:18.221 --> 00:23:20.960
are used and so forth, and
the administrative data

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:23.150
on how it's managed.

00:23:23.150 --> 00:23:25.940
And this can include
rights management-- so

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:28.119
who can access
it, when and how--

00:23:28.119 --> 00:23:30.861
and also how it's
been preserved, how

00:23:30.861 --> 00:23:33.929
it's been archived and stored.

00:23:33.929 --> 00:23:36.789
When we talk about IR
systems, the best example

00:23:36.789 --> 00:23:41.299
is literature indexing
and annotation.

00:23:41.299 --> 00:23:45.480
But metadata exists for
databases of almost any kind

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:51.070
of computerized resource.

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:56.040
One final question
I'll just cover briefly

00:23:56.040 --> 00:24:00.450
is how do we know how well
we've done when we search.

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:02.590
I've actually-- I
and many other people

00:24:02.590 --> 00:24:04.070
who work in
information retrieval--

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:05.820
I was very interested
in this question

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:10.110
and have done research
looking at it.

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:12.340
There's a lot of questions
we can ask if we want

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:13.960
to know how well a search is.

00:24:13.960 --> 00:24:17.179
Is a search system used that
we make available to users?

00:24:17.179 --> 00:24:18.360
Are they satisfied?

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:19.809
A lot of the
research has focused

00:24:19.809 --> 00:24:23.590
on relevant information--
how much relevant information

00:24:23.590 --> 00:24:25.080
did they find.

00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:26.600
Another question we
may ask is did they

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:28.660
complete their desired task.

00:24:28.660 --> 00:24:31.759
And I've looked at that,
mostly from the standpoint

00:24:31.759 --> 00:24:34.549
of clinicians over the years.

00:24:34.549 --> 00:24:36.489
When we're looking for
relevant information,

00:24:36.489 --> 00:24:42.100
there's the well-known measures
of recall and precision,

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:46.679
recall being of the proportion
of materials out there that

00:24:46.679 --> 00:24:50.409
are relevant, the proportion of
content, what do we get back.

00:24:50.409 --> 00:24:53.490
So retrieved that relevant
divided by relevant.

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:57.679
And then perception is the,
of what we actually retrieved,

00:24:57.679 --> 00:24:58.690
how much is relevant.

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:01.860
So retrieved that relevant
divided by retrieved.

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:03.701
And there's a whole
bunch of issues when you

00:25:03.701 --> 00:25:07.789
use these measures in research.

00:25:07.789 --> 00:25:11.440
And so I won't go
into that detail,

00:25:11.440 --> 00:25:15.009
but you can search
the literature

00:25:15.009 --> 00:25:17.749
in medicine, computer
science for all kinds of work

00:25:17.749 --> 00:25:20.710
that's been done in this area.

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:23.600
So let me-- I'm hoping to
leave a little bit of time

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:24.600
for questions.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:28.370
And so what I was going to spend
the rest of the lecture doing

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:32.590
was going through the
topics of section one.

00:25:32.590 --> 00:25:38.259
And again I'm going to present
it to you, asking questions.

00:25:38.259 --> 00:25:42.399
And I know each of the lectures
in the following five weeks

00:25:42.399 --> 00:25:43.470
will go into details.

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:45.639
And maybe this,
for you, could set

00:25:45.639 --> 00:25:47.789
the stage for what
you might want

00:25:47.789 --> 00:25:52.499
to hear when they go into much
more detail on each of these.

00:25:52.499 --> 00:25:57.539
So next week's lecture is
entitled Finding and Accessing

00:25:57.539 --> 00:25:59.460
Datasets, Indexing
and Identifiers, so

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:02.070
a lot of what I've
been talking about,

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:05.000
but focused on data collections.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:09.830
And there's a number of emerging
sites that are basically

00:26:09.830 --> 00:26:11.730
search engines for data now.

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:14.220
And I list two
here, although I can

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:15.889
think of many more
off the top of head.

00:26:15.889 --> 00:26:19.779
But DataSite was one
of the first ones.

00:26:19.779 --> 00:26:22.610
It's a way to find datasets.

00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:25.309
And DataSite is not
limited to medicine.

00:26:25.309 --> 00:26:29.330
It covers many topics.

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:31.299
There's another one
that's more focused

00:26:31.299 --> 00:26:35.139
on medicine from the
bioCADDIE Project, which

00:26:35.139 --> 00:26:39.559
I believe the speaker next
week is from that project.

00:26:39.559 --> 00:26:45.130
Datamed.org, that lets you
search for collections of data.

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:48.539
And why would you
want to find data?

00:26:48.539 --> 00:26:50.779
Usually because you may be
interested in doing research

00:26:50.779 --> 00:26:51.779
with it.

00:26:51.779 --> 00:26:55.450
You want to replicate
the results of a study

00:26:55.450 --> 00:26:58.389
from the data that they
collected and so forth.

00:26:58.389 --> 00:27:03.659
And so I think the question to
think about here is, I think,

00:27:03.659 --> 00:27:07.779
most information retrieval has
been done with information.

00:27:07.779 --> 00:27:10.690
And as we move from
information to data,

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:14.600
are there any issues
that become unique?

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:19.640
It turns out there are with
datasets in terms of-- whereas,

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:22.740
for example, a journal article's
published, and then it's done.

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:26.029
Maybe there might be
an erratum sometimes,

00:27:26.029 --> 00:27:27.549
or perhaps it's even retracted.

00:27:27.549 --> 00:27:31.759
But by and large, it
becomes a static document.

00:27:31.759 --> 00:27:33.929
Datasets might not
always be static.

00:27:33.929 --> 00:27:36.749
They might have snapshots
at different times.

00:27:36.749 --> 00:27:38.509
But those are some
of the things we

00:27:38.509 --> 00:27:43.039
need to know when we're creating
data retrieval systems to set

00:27:43.039 --> 00:27:46.909
up and allow the searcher
to be able to search

00:27:46.909 --> 00:27:52.330
on those sorts of attributes.

00:27:52.330 --> 00:27:55.539
Another issue, or
the second topic,

00:27:55.539 --> 00:27:59.200
will be data curation
and version control.

00:27:59.200 --> 00:28:03.520
Again, data curation
can sometimes

00:28:03.520 --> 00:28:05.600
become a little more
challenging than, say,

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:10.230
just journal articles because
datasets have a context

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:12.200
and they may grow or change.

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:14.889
But it's important
to remember that data

00:28:14.889 --> 00:28:17.109
doesn't exist in a vacuum.

00:28:17.109 --> 00:28:20.019
Data gets collected
when there's usually

00:28:20.019 --> 00:28:24.710
some sort of experiments
or observation going on.

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:28.539
The only data in the
dataset that is collected

00:28:28.539 --> 00:28:31.789
is that which was collected.

00:28:31.789 --> 00:28:36.419
And so, sometimes, when
people do experiments,

00:28:36.419 --> 00:28:39.700
they may not collect
certain types

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:42.580
of data that actually
could have been collected,

00:28:42.580 --> 00:28:44.770
or perhaps they
just couldn't have

00:28:44.770 --> 00:28:46.090
been collected because
they didn't think

00:28:46.090 --> 00:28:48.390
about collecting those things.

00:28:48.390 --> 00:28:51.210
Probably every researcher has
done at least one experiment

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:54.359
in their lives when they
think to themselves, oh,

00:28:54.359 --> 00:28:56.590
why didn't I measure
this or that when

00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:59.139
we were doing the experiment.

00:28:59.139 --> 00:29:04.110
So the figure here, it
comes from University

00:29:04.110 --> 00:29:07.149
of California Merced website.

00:29:07.149 --> 00:29:10.269
I was looking for a good picture
of research data curation,

00:29:10.269 --> 00:29:11.790
and I found this one.

00:29:11.790 --> 00:29:16.539
And this really
highlights the challenges.

00:29:16.539 --> 00:29:22.169
When we do an experiment, we
need a data management plan.

00:29:22.169 --> 00:29:25.570
In fact, NIH grants
now require that,

00:29:25.570 --> 00:29:28.239
and there's a lot of detail
that goes into storage.

00:29:28.239 --> 00:29:33.570
We collect the data, we have
to describe it, analyze it.

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:36.549
And, eventually,
when we're at least

00:29:36.549 --> 00:29:38.159
done with the
initial experiments,

00:29:38.159 --> 00:29:42.999
we move on then to archiving
the data and publishing.

00:29:42.999 --> 00:29:45.489
Now, historically,
researchers have

00:29:45.489 --> 00:29:49.770
archived data in their own
files, hopefully at least

00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:53.059
electronically, though
that's not always the case.

00:29:53.059 --> 00:29:56.499
Probably not so much anymore.

00:29:56.499 --> 00:30:00.309
But, oftentimes, those
archives are private.

00:30:00.309 --> 00:30:04.279
The study is published,
although, increasingly,

00:30:04.279 --> 00:30:06.440
researchers have to
publish their data,

00:30:06.440 --> 00:30:09.549
especially in the
omics world, less so

00:30:09.549 --> 00:30:11.669
in the clinical
world, though we're

00:30:11.669 --> 00:30:14.990
seeing a lot of conversation
going on about that.

00:30:14.990 --> 00:30:19.039
And of course, as research is
done with extractions of data

00:30:19.039 --> 00:30:22.190
from electronic health records
and other clinical data

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:27.169
sources, not to mention all
of the personal devices,

00:30:27.169 --> 00:30:29.100
wearables and stuff
that people have.

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:32.840
So lots of data.

00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:37.470
Usually the research
question comes

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:39.600
at the beginning of the study.

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:42.379
And I suspect that's
what's actually meant here.

00:30:42.379 --> 00:30:48.249
But again, when we get data,
we then can ask new questions.

00:30:48.249 --> 00:30:51.500
Now we always have to be
careful because, again, data

00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:53.470
was collected for
a certain purpose,

00:30:53.470 --> 00:30:55.539
back to that issue of context.

00:30:55.539 --> 00:30:57.789
But we may have a new
research question.

00:30:57.789 --> 00:31:00.970
And so then, we search the data
and pull out the data elements

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:02.809
we need and reuse them.

00:31:02.809 --> 00:31:06.919
And of course, we need to keep
an eye to reproducibility.

00:31:06.919 --> 00:31:09.919
So if we go through this cycle
again with a new research

00:31:09.919 --> 00:31:12.919
question, we have to
archive the data in a way

00:31:12.919 --> 00:31:16.129
that we can reproduce
the analysis that we've

00:31:16.129 --> 00:31:17.690
done with the data.

00:31:17.690 --> 00:31:21.859
So when you hear the lecture
on data curation and version

00:31:21.859 --> 00:31:23.799
control, you can
think about it, again,

00:31:23.799 --> 00:31:31.419
how the data are described
and kept up to date.

00:31:31.419 --> 00:31:36.729
The third topic you'll hear in
this series is on ontologies.

00:31:36.729 --> 00:31:38.600
And this is actually
another example

00:31:38.600 --> 00:31:43.919
of pulling some materials
from our resources

00:31:43.919 --> 00:31:46.330
in our open
educational resources.

00:31:46.330 --> 00:31:49.269
So this figure comes
from one of our modules

00:31:49.269 --> 00:31:53.019
that's authored by Melissa
Haendel, some of you know,

00:31:53.019 --> 00:31:56.309
who does a lot of
work in ontologies.

00:31:56.309 --> 00:32:02.120
So in case you don't
know what an ontology is.

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:05.249
As it's defined in
our module and it's

00:32:05.249 --> 00:32:08.999
defined similarly elsewhere,
a formal conceptualization

00:32:08.999 --> 00:32:10.840
of a specified domain.

00:32:10.840 --> 00:32:14.509
So clearly the terms
need to be defined.

00:32:14.509 --> 00:32:16.799
Sometimes people
call things like MeSH

00:32:16.799 --> 00:32:20.240
and other control
vocabularies an ontology.

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:22.190
I think the purists
would actually

00:32:22.190 --> 00:32:24.259
argue that they're not.

00:32:24.259 --> 00:32:31.500
MeSH is a fine,
medical vocabulary,

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:33.049
comprehensive
collection of terms

00:32:33.049 --> 00:32:36.419
that are used to describe what's
in the medical literature.

00:32:36.419 --> 00:32:40.779
But an ontology
structures the domain,

00:32:40.779 --> 00:32:42.230
so you understand
the relationship.

00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:45.559
So for example, you
see here that we're

00:32:45.559 --> 00:32:50.299
looking at certain
types of neural cells.

00:32:50.299 --> 00:32:59.350
And those cells or child are
more refined from the ones

00:32:59.350 --> 00:33:02.879
higher in the diagram.

00:33:02.879 --> 00:33:04.460
But there's a
certain type a cell.

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:08.600
So the hippocampal astrocyte
is capable of certain things.

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:11.409
And from a different
part of the ontology,

00:33:11.409 --> 00:33:14.749
one that-- in fact this is
from the gene ontology--

00:33:14.749 --> 00:33:17.519
describes specific functions.

00:33:17.519 --> 00:33:22.169
And so the hippocampal astrocyte
is capable of those functions.

00:33:22.169 --> 00:33:24.600
It also may have
some unique parts.

00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:27.720
And it's also part of organs.

00:33:27.720 --> 00:33:29.640
So for example, it's
part of the brain,

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:31.470
or it's also part of
the hippocampus, which

00:33:31.470 --> 00:33:33.090
is part of the brain.

00:33:33.090 --> 00:33:36.840
And then there are some
further refinements.

00:33:36.840 --> 00:33:40.350
So in an ontology,
the relationships

00:33:40.350 --> 00:33:42.799
between all the
terms are defined.

00:33:42.799 --> 00:33:45.659
And this not only
organizes the data,

00:33:45.659 --> 00:33:48.309
but it allows us to
make logical inference.

00:33:48.309 --> 00:33:52.600
If, for example, a hippocampal
astrocyte is a cell,

00:33:52.600 --> 00:33:55.190
there are certain
attributes of cell

00:33:55.190 --> 00:34:02.539
that can be inherited down the
hierarchy that we can infer.

00:34:02.539 --> 00:34:08.419
And this enables us to do more
sophisticated data queries.

00:34:08.419 --> 00:34:13.669
Ontologies can be complex,
but they may provide

00:34:13.669 --> 00:34:15.929
a much richer description.

00:34:15.929 --> 00:34:18.539
And so the questions you
might ask with ontologies

00:34:18.539 --> 00:34:24.369
is how we can optimally
index and then do retrieval,

00:34:24.369 --> 00:34:27.549
using ontologies when we're
looking for data, but not

00:34:27.549 --> 00:34:28.639
only for data.

00:34:28.639 --> 00:34:30.849
Can we use these
also for looking

00:34:30.849 --> 00:34:33.169
for literature and
other kinds of things

00:34:33.169 --> 00:34:37.690
if they've been indexed
using an ontology?

00:34:37.690 --> 00:34:44.149
The week that follows will be
taught on metadata standards.

00:34:44.149 --> 00:34:46.990
There are many metadata
standards to choose from.

00:34:46.990 --> 00:34:48.770
And sometimes they
duplicate each other,

00:34:48.770 --> 00:34:50.200
which can be a problem.

00:34:50.200 --> 00:34:53.099
But oftentimes they're
different because the task

00:34:53.099 --> 00:34:57.250
that the metadata is
put to is different.

00:34:57.250 --> 00:35:02.049
There's actually a standards
body, the International

00:35:02.049 --> 00:35:05.089
Organization for
Standards, or ISO,

00:35:05.089 --> 00:35:10.680
that actually has-- ISO is
the international standards

00:35:10.680 --> 00:35:13.670
body of standards for many,
many things, one of which

00:35:13.670 --> 00:35:14.670
is metadata.

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:17.109
There are standards for
metadata repositories

00:35:17.109 --> 00:35:19.770
and other sorts of things.

00:35:19.770 --> 00:35:22.250
In the information
retrieval arena,

00:35:22.250 --> 00:35:25.890
there are a number of
metadata standards.

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:27.690
The Medline record
when we search

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:30.839
PubMed, or there's other
ways to search Medline.

00:35:30.839 --> 00:35:34.410
The element, the fields,
in that Medline record

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:39.600
are metadata elements, so
the author names, the title,

00:35:39.600 --> 00:35:43.750
the MeSH terms, the
secondary source identifiers.

00:35:43.750 --> 00:35:48.810
All those things are
part of the metadata.

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:51.630
Medline is basically a
collection of metadata records

00:35:51.630 --> 00:35:54.500
for the medical literature.

00:35:54.500 --> 00:35:56.859
Another method that a
standard, some of you

00:35:56.859 --> 00:36:01.640
may be familiar with is
Dublin Core Metadata.

00:36:01.640 --> 00:36:04.150
Dublin Core Metadata
got its start

00:36:04.150 --> 00:36:06.570
in the relatively early
days of the World Wide Web

00:36:06.570 --> 00:36:08.430
in the 1990s.

00:36:08.430 --> 00:36:13.560
It originally-- well, actually
still consists of 15 fields

00:36:13.560 --> 00:36:16.400
that are actually have--
some of them have analogues

00:36:16.400 --> 00:36:18.670
to what's in Medline--
so the creator,

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:24.940
the name of the resource, the
URI, which maps to the URL.

00:36:24.940 --> 00:36:26.270
There's a field called subject.

00:36:26.270 --> 00:36:30.270
There's also a
field called rights

00:36:30.270 --> 00:36:35.040
that lists the rights management
in terms of who can access it.

00:36:35.040 --> 00:36:38.839
And Dublin Core Metadata has
been extended, really built

00:36:38.839 --> 00:36:43.160
on top of, by many, many
groups, one of which

00:36:43.160 --> 00:36:47.369
is DataSite, which
I've already mentioned.

00:36:47.369 --> 00:36:52.670
They have a metadata
for each data source.

00:36:52.670 --> 00:36:57.240
And their system
for metadata builds

00:36:57.240 --> 00:37:00.410
on top of the Dublin Core.

00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:03.520
And then also groups
like bioCADDIE

00:37:03.520 --> 00:37:07.760
that map among a variety
of metadata standards

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:09.579
with the focus on
biomedical data.

00:37:09.579 --> 00:37:13.350
So whereas Medline has the
focus on biomedical literature,

00:37:13.350 --> 00:37:16.960
Dublin Core had the focus
originally on web resources,

00:37:16.960 --> 00:37:20.770
mainly HTML pages, but
expanding to other things,

00:37:20.770 --> 00:37:23.400
bioCADDIE is focused
on biomedical data.

00:37:23.400 --> 00:37:27.740
So one of the questions
you might ask in that talk

00:37:27.740 --> 00:37:30.750
is what approaches
to metadata are

00:37:30.750 --> 00:37:36.800
optimal for data indexing,
all the kinds of things

00:37:36.800 --> 00:37:39.299
we might want to ask
about data resources

00:37:39.299 --> 00:37:41.180
when we're trying
to retrieve them.

00:37:41.180 --> 00:37:49.109
And then, of course, how do
we build retrieval pages.

00:37:49.109 --> 00:37:56.599
Datamed.org is one approach.

00:37:56.599 --> 00:37:59.650
And I know it's a
work in progress.

00:37:59.650 --> 00:38:03.160
And what fields do they need to
add, what search capabilities,

00:38:03.160 --> 00:38:05.490
and so forth.

00:38:05.490 --> 00:38:13.190
The final topic in this
section is provenance.

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:16.440
And data provenance
is important.

00:38:16.440 --> 00:38:18.630
Basically, data
provenance-- what is it?

00:38:18.630 --> 00:38:20.960
It's where your data come from.

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:23.859
And whenever I talk
about data provenance,

00:38:23.859 --> 00:38:25.510
I always like to
give an example.

00:38:25.510 --> 00:38:29.920
And a few years ago, some
clinical informatics colleagues

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:33.089
and I published the paper
and some of the challenges

00:38:33.089 --> 00:38:37.309
with using clinical data
from electronic health

00:38:37.309 --> 00:38:39.790
records in research.

00:38:39.790 --> 00:38:42.280
And just a number
of caveats, in fact

00:38:42.280 --> 00:38:44.049
that's the title of
the paper, Caveats

00:38:44.049 --> 00:38:47.810
for Using Clinical
Data in Research.

00:38:47.810 --> 00:38:51.289
And provenance is one
of the big issues.

00:38:51.289 --> 00:38:53.599
And I think this
will be an example.

00:38:53.599 --> 00:38:58.059
And there's analogous examples
from laboratory experiments

00:38:58.059 --> 00:38:59.160
and so forth.

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:01.260
But a simple
question we might ask

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:02.819
when we have a
clinical data source--

00:39:02.819 --> 00:39:04.460
is a patient on a medication?

00:39:04.460 --> 00:39:06.260
Are they taking a medication?

00:39:06.260 --> 00:39:12.270
Well, there's actually multiple
ways that that's expressed,

00:39:12.270 --> 00:39:14.760
and we really need to
understand each one and also

00:39:14.760 --> 00:39:18.109
the limitations,
because sometimes

00:39:18.109 --> 00:39:21.579
the physician might just write
I'm putting a patient

00:39:21.579 --> 00:39:23.150
on this medication,
but there might not be an

00:39:23.150 --> 00:39:26.480
order or prescription for it.

00:39:26.480 --> 00:39:29.301
So once the order is
made, then the data

00:39:29.301 --> 00:39:31.830
gets sent to a pharmacy,
either in the hospital

00:39:31.830 --> 00:39:36.870
or if it's an outpatient to
a pharmacy in the community.

00:39:36.870 --> 00:39:40.079
But again, there's not 100
percent of prescriptions

00:39:40.079 --> 00:39:41.859
that make that journey.

00:39:41.859 --> 00:39:46.160
Of course, they end up in
the pharmacy due to does

00:39:46.160 --> 00:39:48.940
the patient actually get
the prescription filled,

00:39:48.940 --> 00:39:50.960
and then actually do they
take the medications.

00:39:50.960 --> 00:39:53.859
And in the hospitals, you
have things like medication

00:39:53.859 --> 00:39:56.280
administration records.

00:39:56.280 --> 00:39:59.740
And then also when, these
days, patients come back

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:01.579
to hospitals, we
have to do a process

00:40:01.579 --> 00:40:03.970
of medication reconciliation.

00:40:03.970 --> 00:40:07.589
So the point of this
is to really show

00:40:07.589 --> 00:40:12.910
that there is many truths--
and I put truth in quote-- for

00:40:12.910 --> 00:40:14.960
whether or not a patient
is on a medication,

00:40:14.960 --> 00:40:17.079
and that can be really important
if we're doing a research

00:40:17.079 --> 00:40:20.170
study where we're trying
to process the data that's

00:40:20.170 --> 00:40:23.940
in an electronic health record.

00:40:23.940 --> 00:40:28.529
And certainly any reuse of
data must take provenance

00:40:28.529 --> 00:40:29.960
into account.

00:40:29.960 --> 00:40:33.480
So I'm sure that those of
you who are more biologically

00:40:33.480 --> 00:40:35.710
inclined can come
up with examples

00:40:35.710 --> 00:40:39.520
like the one I've
given here of how

00:40:39.520 --> 00:40:41.210
do we know what the
truth is, how do we

00:40:41.210 --> 00:40:43.819
know where the data comes from.

00:40:43.819 --> 00:40:51.000
And so when you're listening to
the lecture on data provenance,

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:53.720
you can ask things
like how do we best

00:40:53.720 --> 00:40:58.190
describe the provenance
that's in datasets.

00:40:58.190 --> 00:41:02.390
So at this point, I'm
going to continue.

00:41:02.390 --> 00:41:06.380
We wanted to leave some
time for questions,

00:41:06.380 --> 00:41:11.190
and I'm happy to
answer any questions.

00:41:11.190 --> 00:41:12.539
As I understand it,
people can either

00:41:12.539 --> 00:41:18.529
raise their hands electronically
or type questions into the box.

00:41:18.529 --> 00:41:21.970
Dr. Jack Van Horn: Thank you, Bill.

00:41:21.970 --> 00:41:23.210
So there's a couple
of questions that

00:41:23.210 --> 00:41:26.910
are in the box somebody
has asked about.

00:41:26.910 --> 00:41:29.910
They're asking about the
storage and retrieval

00:41:29.910 --> 00:41:32.590
of experimental
data, also, are there

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:36.609
any standards or common
structures like HDF5.

00:41:36.609 --> 00:41:39.670
And how does this relate
to open data sharing?

00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:42.750
Dr. William Hersh: Yeah.

00:41:42.750 --> 00:41:46.849
I have to admit I'm not
an expert in storage

00:41:46.849 --> 00:41:49.079
of biological data.

00:41:49.079 --> 00:41:51.910
I do know that
probably that area

00:41:51.910 --> 00:41:56.980
is more developed than storage
of things like clinical trials

00:41:56.980 --> 00:41:58.910
data, which may
sometimes just be

00:41:58.910 --> 00:42:03.430
in very ad hoc kinds of ways.

00:42:03.430 --> 00:42:07.880
That question, especially on
the specific data storage,

00:42:07.880 --> 00:42:13.460
I would probably need
to defer to someone,

00:42:13.460 --> 00:42:15.440
and maybe one of the
lecturers in the coming week

00:42:15.440 --> 00:42:16.930
would be able to answer that.

00:42:16.930 --> 00:42:20.539
Jack Van Horn: There was also the
question about how this all

00:42:20.539 --> 00:42:23.589
relates to open data
sharing initiatives.

00:42:23.589 --> 00:42:28.140
Dr. William Hersh: Yeah, sure.

00:42:28.140 --> 00:42:33.289
Well, I think that open data is
actually really, in most ways,

00:42:33.289 --> 00:42:36.710
really separate from
how you store the data.

00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:39.420
When data becomes open,
the question, of course,

00:42:39.420 --> 00:42:42.020
is how open it is.

00:42:42.020 --> 00:42:44.799
Is it just sitting there freely
available on the internet

00:42:44.799 --> 00:42:50.510
or do you have to file a
request with the originator

00:42:50.510 --> 00:42:51.539
of the data?

00:42:51.539 --> 00:42:55.130
If there is potentially
identifiable data

00:42:55.130 --> 00:42:58.359
in there on people?

00:42:58.359 --> 00:43:03.579
Do you have to go through
some sort of IRB process?

00:43:03.579 --> 00:43:08.089
I think it all depends.

00:43:08.089 --> 00:43:10.890
I'm an advocate of
open data, but I also

00:43:10.890 --> 00:43:13.520
understand that
we need to protect

00:43:13.520 --> 00:43:19.470
researchers who create the data
and know the context of it.

00:43:19.470 --> 00:43:20.859
They should be
willing to share it,

00:43:20.859 --> 00:43:25.450
but they also have some interest
in getting the most out of it.

00:43:25.450 --> 00:43:31.660
And then, also, when we start
to get into data on people,

00:43:31.660 --> 00:43:35.119
especially when we start
massively sequencing genomes

00:43:35.119 --> 00:43:39.310
and if we don't have
ways to keep that data

00:43:39.310 --> 00:43:43.109
private and deidentified.

00:43:43.109 --> 00:43:44.710
We have to careful
for those things.

00:43:44.710 --> 00:43:46.380
There's actually a
number of studies

00:43:46.380 --> 00:43:47.770
that it's been shown,
sometimes, depending

00:43:47.770 --> 00:43:52.089
on what data you
have, reidentify

00:43:52.089 --> 00:43:55.200
people from so-called
deidentified datasets.

00:43:55.200 --> 00:43:59.309
Dr. Jack Van Horn: It also looks like, Bill,
we

00:43:59.309 --> 00:44:02.250
have some library scientists
out there.

00:44:02.250 --> 00:44:05.230
And so a couple of them
have asked questions about

00:44:05.230 --> 00:44:07.950
are librarians and
library scientists

00:44:07.950 --> 00:44:10.300
being included on
data management teams,

00:44:10.300 --> 00:44:11.549
and should they be?

00:44:11.549 --> 00:44:14.030
And also how does the
ontological structures

00:44:14.030 --> 00:44:17.289
that you've described-- how
do they link in with MeSH,

00:44:17.289 --> 00:44:18.410
for example?

00:44:18.410 --> 00:44:20.890
Dr. William Hersh: Sure.

00:44:20.890 --> 00:44:23.930
I certainly believe
that librarians

00:44:23.930 --> 00:44:30.549
bring important skills to
the groups that manage data.

00:44:30.549 --> 00:44:36.809
I don't think that researchers
always realize the-- you always

00:44:36.809 --> 00:44:39.460
hear stories about researchers
who lost their old data,

00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:43.510
or they can't figure
out what the data was.

00:44:43.510 --> 00:44:47.900
And that's why it's
important to bring those

00:44:47.900 --> 00:44:50.380
with the kind of
expertise that librarians

00:44:50.380 --> 00:44:59.210
have to make sure that your
data is documented well.

00:44:59.210 --> 00:45:04.950
Metadata is there to define
what your data elements are so

00:45:04.950 --> 00:45:09.220
that you have good
definitions and can go back

00:45:09.220 --> 00:45:11.640
to your data in the future
through the metadata

00:45:11.640 --> 00:45:16.370
to remember exactly
what the data is.

00:45:16.370 --> 00:45:19.020
So there is absolutely
a role for those sorts.

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:21.010
And the second
question, again was?

00:45:21.010 --> 00:45:24.970
Dr. Jack Van Horn: How do the
ontological structures

00:45:24.970 --> 00:45:28.140
that you've described-- how
might they link in with MeSH?

00:45:28.140 --> 00:45:32.049
Dr. William Hersh: Sure.

00:45:32.049 --> 00:45:35.630
I showed you the
ontology of the cells

00:45:35.630 --> 00:45:40.190
and the certain
neural cells types.

00:45:40.190 --> 00:45:44.430
The individual items
in the ontology

00:45:44.430 --> 00:45:50.329
should be in controlled
terminology, sets like MeSH

00:45:50.329 --> 00:45:52.150
or other ones.

00:45:52.150 --> 00:45:55.760
MeSH tends to go into more
details around diseases.

00:45:55.760 --> 00:46:00.299
It tends to have more of a focus
on the biomedical literature.

00:46:00.299 --> 00:46:03.230
But there's other
controlled vocabulary sets.

00:46:03.230 --> 00:46:06.230
So the terms in
an ontology should

00:46:06.230 --> 00:46:10.789
be from a controlled
vocabulary set.

00:46:10.789 --> 00:46:14.779
And then if something,
say, indexes the literature

00:46:14.779 --> 00:46:19.630
like the way that
MeSH does that you could then

00:46:19.630 --> 00:46:23.940
map-- even though MeSH doesn't
capture those relationships.

00:46:23.940 --> 00:46:26.849
But you would at least be
able to map from the terms.

00:46:26.849 --> 00:46:29.440
And of course there's
another important resource

00:46:29.440 --> 00:46:33.700
here that we should mention,
which is the Unified Medical

00:46:33.700 --> 00:46:38.339
Language System, or UMLS, that's
been developed by the National

00:46:38.339 --> 00:46:39.980
Library of Medicine.

00:46:39.980 --> 00:46:45.190
The UMLS is a collection
of terminologies,

00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:49.720
and there are basically synonym
linkages across vocabularies.

00:46:49.720 --> 00:46:53.510
So if you find a term in
one control vocabulary

00:46:53.510 --> 00:46:58.180
and it occurs in another, you
can use the UMLS meta-thesaurus

00:46:58.180 --> 00:47:03.390
to map across those
terminologies sets.

00:47:03.390 --> 00:47:07.609
So again, the terms
in an ontology

00:47:07.609 --> 00:47:09.830
should definitely be from
a controlled vocabulary.

00:47:09.830 --> 00:47:12.710
If they're not, they
should become part of one.

00:47:12.710 --> 00:47:14.480
Dr. Jack Van Horn: Great.

00:47:14.480 --> 00:47:17.430
We had a question
that you had mentioned

00:47:17.430 --> 00:47:20.130
that you'd like to see more
clinical data being deposited

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:22.279
into open repositories.

00:47:22.279 --> 00:47:24.800
And could you possibly
speak about the time frame

00:47:24.800 --> 00:47:26.589
that you might see
this happening in

00:47:26.589 --> 00:47:29.059
and which stakeholders
might drive it?

00:47:29.059 --> 00:47:31.549
And also maybe comment
about how so doing would

00:47:31.549 --> 00:47:35.000
help to encourage
reproducibility,

00:47:35.000 --> 00:47:38.910
avoid misrepresentation
of data, and the like?

00:47:38.910 --> 00:47:41.910
Dr. William Hersh: Yeah.

00:47:41.910 --> 00:47:45.809
Well, I certainly support the
notion that data should be open

00:47:45.809 --> 00:47:47.271
and people should
have access to it,

00:47:47.271 --> 00:47:50.650
especially data
that's being collected

00:47:50.650 --> 00:47:53.100
on projects that are
funded by the government,

00:47:53.100 --> 00:47:54.250
by our tax dollars.

00:47:54.250 --> 00:47:58.010
I do, though, think
we need to be careful.

00:47:58.010 --> 00:48:00.050
And so there probably
needs to be a process.

00:48:00.050 --> 00:48:02.670
I know, for example, in
some of the NLM databases

00:48:02.670 --> 00:48:06.039
that have the genome
wide association studies,

00:48:06.039 --> 00:48:11.049
there are processes where you
have to apply to get data.

00:48:11.049 --> 00:48:13.920
The two things I think we
need to be careful with.

00:48:13.920 --> 00:48:15.660
I don't think that
researchers should

00:48:15.660 --> 00:48:17.900
be able to hoard their
data, but researchers

00:48:17.900 --> 00:48:22.839
know a lot of the context of
the experiments that they run.

00:48:22.839 --> 00:48:27.300
They spent a lot of time and
effort collecting the data.

00:48:27.300 --> 00:48:30.791
So they should certainly be able
to get the most out of the data

00:48:30.791 --> 00:48:33.039
when it's first generated,
the publications.

00:48:33.039 --> 00:48:39.569
So certainly there should be
some period where they have

00:48:39.569 --> 00:48:41.450
total purview over the data.

00:48:41.450 --> 00:48:44.230
Then, when it's a
reasonable amount of time--

00:48:44.230 --> 00:48:48.140
and you can argue how long,
six months, 12 months, some

00:48:48.140 --> 00:48:51.720
say things like five years,
which is probably too long--

00:48:51.720 --> 00:48:55.049
then it should go into some
controlled, released sort

00:48:55.049 --> 00:48:56.049
of format.

00:48:56.049 --> 00:48:58.289
I think it's especially
going to be true

00:48:58.289 --> 00:49:05.299
as we do more things with
people that identify them,

00:49:05.299 --> 00:49:06.839
their genome.

00:49:06.839 --> 00:49:09.069
I know that there
are some methods--

00:49:09.069 --> 00:49:14.760
and I'm no expert in them--
to partially de-identify a gene

00:49:14.760 --> 00:49:16.240
variance and things like that.

00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:20.289
But the reality is
that there will be--

00:49:20.289 --> 00:49:23.000
and people showing you
can even-- the famous

00:49:23.000 --> 00:49:26.390
study by Latanya Sweeney,
where she was able to identify

00:49:26.390 --> 00:49:30.019
the governor of Massachusetts
in a health database

00:49:30.019 --> 00:49:32.549
by purchasing the
voter registration

00:49:32.549 --> 00:49:35.910
rolls for the city of
Cambridge, Massachusetts

00:49:35.910 --> 00:49:38.550
and connecting him
through his-- I

00:49:38.550 --> 00:49:44.069
think it was zip code,
gender, and date of birth.

00:49:44.069 --> 00:49:47.520
So data should be open,
but it should be released

00:49:47.520 --> 00:49:49.570
in a way that's controlled.

00:49:49.570 --> 00:49:52.150
And I think there's
a role for IRBs

00:49:52.150 --> 00:49:56.150
here to approve
these sorts of things

00:49:56.150 --> 00:50:00.400
because there's always
another potential of someone

00:50:00.400 --> 00:50:04.560
doing an analysis that is
not really justified based

00:50:04.560 --> 00:50:08.559
on the context of the data
and getting different results.

00:50:08.559 --> 00:50:11.380
I think there are still issues
to be worked out especially

00:50:11.380 --> 00:50:14.400
when we start releasing
data that's originally

00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:16.000
collected by humans.

00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:19.160
But I certainly support
the notion of doing that.

00:50:19.160 --> 00:50:22.789
Dr. Jack Van Horn: There was a question
about what does

00:50:22.789 --> 00:50:25.510
the lack of consistent
ontologies across all

00:50:25.510 --> 00:50:28.390
the medical domains from
practice to research and back

00:50:28.390 --> 00:50:29.390
again.

00:50:29.390 --> 00:50:32.250
Does that come at a
cost to the country,

00:50:32.250 --> 00:50:34.099
and could you comment on that?

00:50:34.099 --> 00:50:36.339
Dr. William Hersh: [LAUGHS] Yeah.

00:50:36.339 --> 00:50:40.160
Well, I can answer the
question simply by saying yes.

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:44.349
But just to make some
additional comments.

00:50:44.349 --> 00:50:46.130
Yes.

00:50:46.130 --> 00:50:49.440
You see this now in the
clinical informatics world,

00:50:49.440 --> 00:50:51.450
where I spend most of my time.

00:50:51.450 --> 00:50:55.589
So we've finally,
after all these years,

00:50:55.589 --> 00:50:58.039
have achieved widespread
adoption of electronic health

00:50:58.039 --> 00:50:59.470
records.

00:50:59.470 --> 00:51:02.789
Unfortunately, they don't
easily talk to each other.

00:51:02.789 --> 00:51:05.850
Even when they can
exchange the bits,

00:51:05.850 --> 00:51:10.099
the electrons of the
data, they don't readily

00:51:10.099 --> 00:51:13.710
exchange the meaning.

00:51:13.710 --> 00:51:17.319
And probably we'll never
achieve perfection on this

00:51:17.319 --> 00:51:20.590
because different humans
interpret different things

00:51:20.590 --> 00:51:24.140
about patients and so forth.

00:51:24.140 --> 00:51:28.040
But a big part of the problem--
and the Office of the National

00:51:28.040 --> 00:51:30.329
Coordinator for Health IT
has recognized this now--

00:51:30.329 --> 00:51:34.180
is that we haven't really
adhered to standards, standards

00:51:34.180 --> 00:51:37.820
onto elements and medical
records, standards on the terms

00:51:37.820 --> 00:51:40.910
that we use to describe
diseases, lab tests, and so

00:51:40.910 --> 00:51:41.910
forth.

00:51:41.910 --> 00:51:44.660
And there are
standards out there.

00:51:44.660 --> 00:51:48.589
They're not all perfect,
but they go a long way.

00:51:48.589 --> 00:51:53.589
So I think that we need
to encourage standards.

00:51:53.589 --> 00:51:56.630
And there's standards
processes that individuals

00:51:56.630 --> 00:51:59.609
who really care about
them can get involved.

00:51:59.609 --> 00:52:01.789
But we need to encourage
standards in a way

00:52:01.789 --> 00:52:05.640
that we call something
the same thing,

00:52:05.640 --> 00:52:07.360
or at least that
there's an identifier.

00:52:07.360 --> 00:52:09.710
We might have different
ways of describing,

00:52:09.710 --> 00:52:12.549
different synonyms for it,
but that they ultimately

00:52:12.549 --> 00:52:14.740
map back to some identifier.

00:52:14.740 --> 00:52:17.769
We should do that
as much as possible.

00:52:17.769 --> 00:52:20.059
There may be some things
that change over time.

00:52:20.059 --> 00:52:22.109
I commonly give an example.

00:52:22.109 --> 00:52:23.380
When I was in
medical school, there

00:52:23.380 --> 00:52:27.589
was no hepatitis C or D or
however many different types

00:52:27.589 --> 00:52:28.589
of hepatitis.

00:52:28.589 --> 00:52:31.210
It was all non-A,
non-B hepatitis.

00:52:31.210 --> 00:52:33.849
And so terminologies
change over time,

00:52:33.849 --> 00:52:39.369
and we need to model
the adaptation of terms.

00:52:39.369 --> 00:52:44.660
But yes, we should strive to
use standards whenever we can.

00:52:44.660 --> 00:52:48.700
It should be required by
the government, the funders,

00:52:48.700 --> 00:52:53.869
the journals, et cetera, so that
we achieve as much use of it

00:52:53.869 --> 00:52:54.869
as possible.

00:52:54.869 --> 00:52:57.849
Dr. Jack Van Horn: Well with that point,
another question

00:52:57.849 --> 00:53:02.319
was would you care to predict
or guess at the chances

00:53:02.319 --> 00:53:04.000
that, in the future, a
literature will become

00:53:04.000 --> 00:53:07.339
Pre-indexed with people
maybe preregistering

00:53:07.339 --> 00:53:10.250
their studies against
particular data ontologies

00:53:10.250 --> 00:53:12.779
or with particular methods.

00:53:12.779 --> 00:53:16.329
Dr. William Hersh: Yeah.

00:53:16.329 --> 00:53:19.529
Well, certainly that
possibility exists.

00:53:19.529 --> 00:53:22.141
In fact, for clinical
trials now, they

00:53:22.141 --> 00:53:24.799
have to be registered
in clinicaltrials.gov.

00:53:24.799 --> 00:53:31.250
And certainly people who
are awarded NIH grants,

00:53:31.250 --> 00:53:33.220
they know what they're studying.

00:53:33.220 --> 00:53:35.070
So they could do
that free indexing.

00:53:35.070 --> 00:53:39.119
Of course sometimes with studies,
you have unexpected findings.

00:53:39.119 --> 00:53:42.710
So I don't know that the
indexing would totally stop

00:53:42.710 --> 00:53:46.970
before the study was completed.

00:53:46.970 --> 00:53:48.880
But that's certainly
a possibility.

00:53:48.880 --> 00:53:56.589
And you can go search databases
like clinicaltrials.gov to find

00:53:56.589 --> 00:54:01.309
studies on specific diseases
and treatments and so forth.

00:54:01.309 --> 00:54:03.980
Dr. Jack Van Horn: I see.

00:54:03.980 --> 00:54:08.859
Do we have any other
questions that are good ones?

00:54:08.859 --> 00:54:10.400
I'm sure there's a
bunch here, and we'll

00:54:10.400 --> 00:54:13.180
be delighted to share
them with you, Bill.

00:54:13.180 --> 00:54:17.450
And maybe you can reach out
to the askers at a later time.

00:54:17.450 --> 00:54:19.000
But it looks like-- sorry?

00:54:19.000 --> 00:54:21.900
Dr. William Hersh: Yes.

00:54:21.900 --> 00:54:22.900
Sure.

00:54:22.900 --> 00:54:24.680
I'd be happy to do that.

00:54:24.680 --> 00:54:27.690
We could even make it
publicly available.

00:54:27.690 --> 00:54:31.049
I can type in the answers to the
extent that I can [INAUDIBLE].

00:54:31.049 --> 00:54:33.980
Dr. Jack Van Horn: Yeah, that would be wonderful.

00:54:33.980 --> 00:54:37.329
Well, let me bring
things to a close

00:54:37.329 --> 00:54:39.359
here and thank Bill
for a fantastic

00:54:39.359 --> 00:54:43.559
lecture in our second of our
data science seminar series.

00:54:43.559 --> 00:54:45.569
Once again, let me
apologize to anybody

00:54:45.569 --> 00:54:48.519
who had some difficulty
getting in initially.

00:54:48.519 --> 00:54:54.000
As it might be clear, we
had a peak of 511 of you.

00:54:54.000 --> 00:54:56.119
And as a consequence,
we had to make sure

00:54:56.119 --> 00:54:58.490
that we could upgrade and
accommodate everybody.

00:54:58.490 --> 00:55:01.599
So we had to update our URL.

00:55:01.599 --> 00:55:04.900
The best way to find
the information is to go

00:55:04.900 --> 00:55:10.730
to www.bigdatayou.o
rg/data-science-seminars

00:55:10.730 --> 00:55:12.450
and follow the
instructions there.

00:55:12.450 --> 00:55:15.960
And so this GoToWebinar
site will be the site

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:17.259
we always come back to.

00:55:17.259 --> 00:55:20.230
So please spread the
word and come back.

00:55:20.230 --> 00:55:22.930
And we'll see you
again next week.

00:55:22.930 --> 00:55:24.750
So thank you very much everyone.

00:55:24.750 --> 00:55:25.230
Have a great day.

